WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.301
NoSQL injection is less about SQL syntax and more about unsafe query structure.

2
00:00:05.301 --> 00:00:20.097
In document databases, search systems, and JSON-driven APIs, user-controlled objects can sometimes influence operators, filters, selectors, or nested conditions if they are passed directly into query construction.

3
00:00:20.097 --> 00:00:25.794
The risk is that the application accepts more structure or behavior than it intended.

4
00:00:25.794 --> 00:00:31.332
JavaScript developers should pay attention to JSON body parsing and object shape.

5
00:00:31.332 --> 00:00:41.539
A request body may look like ordinary data, but it can contain nested objects, arrays, unexpected types, or fields that the route did not intend to expose.

6
00:00:41.539 --> 00:00:50.322
If application code passes that structure directly into a query, update, or filter, the user may influence more than a simple value.

7
00:00:50.322 --> 00:00:54.119
Schema validation and type checking are important here.

8
00:00:54.119 --> 00:01:06.067
Define the expected shape of input, reject unknown fields where appropriate, constrain types, set length limits, and map accepted fields into application-owned query objects.

9
00:01:06.067 --> 00:01:10.735
This is different from simply checking that the request is valid JSON.

10
00:01:10.735 --> 00:01:16.827
JSON syntax only proves the input can be parsed, not that it represents an authorized query.

11
00:01:16.827 --> 00:01:22.208
Allowlisting fields and operators is especially important for filtering APIs.

12
00:01:22.208 --> 00:01:31.069
A client may be allowed to filter by status, owner, or creation date, but not by arbitrary internal fields or special query operators.

13
00:01:31.069 --> 00:01:41.039
The application should translate approved client choices into controlled query behavior rather than treating the request body as the query itself.

14
00:01:41.039 --> 00:01:44.837
Mass assignment and over-posting are related concerns.

15
00:01:44.837 --> 00:01:54.727
If a JavaScript API accepts an entire object and writes or queries with it directly, clients may provide fields the developer did not intend to honor.

16
00:01:54.727 --> 00:02:02.481
The safer pattern is to validate intent, construct explicit objects, and make the allowed behavior visible in code.
